<title>The stability of equilibria of a differential equation</title>

<p><ref>Video 1</ref> | <ref>Video 2</ref> | <ref>Transcripts</ref> </p>

<description>
Identifying stable and unstable equilibria of a differential equation by graphically solving the equation for nearby initial conditions.
</description>

<section><title>Transcripts</title>
<section><title>Video 1</title>

<p>If I have an autonomous differential equation of the form dx/dt = f(x), the equilibria are the points where dx/dt is equal to zero, i.e., where f(x) equals 0. If f of some number, x_e let's call it, is equal to 0, then x(t) being constant equal to that number, x_e, is a solution. This constant solution is an equilibrium. And it’s a solution because d/dt of a constant is 0, and f of that constant is also 0. This means if we have the initial condition that x of some time, let's call it 0, is equal to x_e, then the solution must be x(t) = x_e. If we start at the equilibrium, we stay there for all time. This is just the definition of an equilibrium. </p>

<p>But if we have a different initial condition? What if we have the initial condition x at time 0 is some number, x_0, that is close to x_e, but not exactly equal to the equilibrium x_e? Then what happens? Well there are different possibilities. Maybe x(t) moves away from the equilibrium as t increases. Or maybe, x(t) moves toward the equilibrium. And we’ll include in this second possibility that it at least doesn’t get any further away –  it just stays the same distance away. In this first case, we say that the equilibrium is unstable. </p>

<p>For an unstable equilibrium, if the initial condition is exactly at the equilibrium, we’ll stay there by the definition. But if we start just a little bit away, as time increases, we’ll move away from the equilibrium. This is like the idea of balancing a ball on the top of a hill. If the ball’s exactly at the top, it’ll stay there. But if it jiggles a little bit one way or the other, it's going to go down one side or the other, depending on which way it jiggled.</p> 

<p>In the second case, when x(t) moves toward the equilibrium, we say the equilibrium is stable. A stable equilibrium corresponds to the case where a ball is sitting at the bottom of a valley. If the ball starts exactly at the bottom of the valley it won’t move. But if it starts a little bit away from the bottom, it’ll move back toward the bottom. So the ball moves back toward the equilibrium. </p>

<p>So, when we find an equilibrium we have two cases: the equilibrium might be unstable, or it might be stable. And one important goal when studying dynamical systems such as this differential equation is to find methods to determine the stability of the equilibria.</p> 

<p>In summary, for a differential equation dx/dt = f(x), the equilibria are the points x_e, where f(x_e) = 0, which means dx/dt is 0, and the solution x(t) is x_e for all time. Equilibria can be either unstable or stable. They’re unstable if nearby trajectories or solutions move away, and stable if nearby trajectories move closer (or at least don’t move further away). The next question is how to determine stability? </p>

<p>Let’s start off by thinking about the graphical approach of solving differential equations. In the graphical approach we plot f(x), let’s say it looks something like this, and we use this plot of the function f to determine what plots of x versus t might look like. One important point to remember is that these two graphs are very different. In the plot of f at the left, x is on the horizontal axis, while in the right plot, x is on the vertical axis. The equilibria are points where f(x_e) = 0, so we can find them by looking at the points where f intersects the x-axis. In this case we have 3 equilibria. We can also plot those three equilibria on the right figure. One where x is largest, would be somewhere around here, and the second one x is just below 0, right around here, and the third one x is even more negative. On the left plot it makes sense to draw these equilibria as points, because these are the values of x where f is 0. On the right plot though we’re plotting versus time, and the idea of an equilibrium is that x(t) is at the value x_e, the equilibrium value, for all time. So if we were to plot an equilibrium versus time, it should be a horizontal line, or the plot of a constant function. So this largest equilibrium would be a line like this, the middle equilibrium would be a line like this, and the smallest like that. </p>

<p>The stability of an equilibrium captures what happens to solutions that start nearby the equilibrium. Let’s look at this upper equilibrium first. If I start just above the equilibrium, the question is – do I move toward or away from the equilibrium? Well, looking at my differential equation, dx/dt = f(x), I can see that to determine the velocity dx/dt I just need to look up what f(x) is. Well, for this value of x, f is negative, so that means I must move to the left. So if I start over here to the right I should move with time toward the equilibrium. If I were to plot this versus time on the right I would start up here and as time evolves I should get closer to the equilibrium. What happens if I start just below? Then when I look up my velocity, I see that dx/dt, or f(x) is positive, so I should be moving toward the right. If I start down here below I should move up, and get closer to the equilibrium from below. On the plot versus time it looks something like this. So for the upper equilibrium, nearby trajectories are moving closer, so therefore the upper equilibrium is stable. </p>

<p>How about for the middle equilibrium? If I start at a value just above the middle equilibrium, the velocity f(x) or dx/dt is positive, so I should be moving to the right. If I start over here I should move on toward the right. In fact, no matter how close I start to the equilibrium, f just above the equilibrium will be positive, so again I’ll be moving to the right. I’ll plot that result on the figure at the right; I start here close to the equilibrium, and I move away. And in fact I’ll move to the upper equilibrium, but that doesn’t matter as far as the stability for the middle equilibrium is concerned. Stability is just a local behavior – what happens when you’re nearby an equilibrium? On the other hand, if I start just below the equilibrium, the velocity f(x) or dx/dt is negative, so I’m moving to the left, no matter how close I start to the equilibrium. By plotting this versus time, I move away from the middle equilibrium. Nearby trajectories are moving away, so therefore this middle equilibrium is unstable. </p>

<p>What about the lower equilibrium? Well, I already saw that if I start above, I’ll move closer to it. In particular if I start very close and above I’ll move closer to it, since the nearby trajectories are the important ones for stability. So if I start here right above it I’ll move with time closer to it. And if I start below, f(x) is positive, so dx/dt is positive, and we move to the right, and get closer to the equilibrium with time. The plot with time looks like this. Just as for the upper equilibrium nearby trajectories move closer –  then the lower equilibrium is stable. Just by looking at the graph of the function f(x), we can determine the stability of the equilibria of dx/dt = f(x). We can also determine the stability analytically, which will be the subject of the next video. </p>
</section>

<section><title>Video 2</title>

<p>For an autonomous differential equation, dx/dt = f(x), the equilibria are the points, x_e let’s say, where f(x_e) = 0, and in the previous video we learned how to determine the stability of the equilibria using the graphical approach. By looking at the graph of the function f, we could determine whether or not initial conditions that are near the equilibrium (**move toward the equilibrium**) – in which case it would be stable, or move away from the equilibrium, in which case it would be unstable.</p> 

<p>Let’s see now how we could determine the stability of the equilibrium analytically, without drawing a graph. To do this let’s start with the simplest type of autonomous differential equation, a linear one, of the form dx/dt = ax, where a is a parameter. “a” is just some number. And let’s assume that a is not equal to 0, because if a = 0 then every point is an equilibrium, because dx/dt would be equal to 0 for any x. If a is not equal to 0, then this equation has only one equilibrium – the equilibrium x = 0. What about the stability of that equilibrium? </p>

<p>Well, if a is less than 0, we have exponential decay, because we know the solution to this linear differential equation – the solution is x(t) equals some constant times e to the at. 
And if a is negative, x(t) decays towards 0. This means the equilibrium is stable.</p> 

<p>On the other hand, if a is positive, this means we have exponential growth. If our initial condition isn’t 0 (**is greater than 0***), then the solution x(t) will blow up, and the equilibrium is unstable.  For the linear dynamical system, dx/dt = ax all that matters for the stability of the equilibrium 0 is the sign of the parameter a. And this is consistent with our graphical approach we learned earlier, the linear system corresponds to f(x) = ax, which if we were to plot it would look like this if a were negative, and would look like this if a were positive. When a is negative, f is negative for positive values of x, and so for positive values of x, x decreases. For negative values of x, f is positive so x increases. In both cases the trajectory x(t) moves towards the equilibrium x = 0. So a plot versus time would look something like this, if the initial condition was positive, or like this if the initial condition was negative. On the other hand if a is positive, if x is positive, f is also positive, so x increases and moves away from the equilibrium x = 0. For negative x, dx/dt is negative since f is negative, so x decreases moving away from the equilibrium x = 0. </p>

<p>So then a plot versus time of the solution would be exponential growth to large positive numbers if the initial condition was positive, and exponential growth to large negative numbers if the initial condition was negative. A key observation for the function f(x) = ax is that the number a is the slope of the graph of the function. So we could say the equilibrium is stable if the graph has a negative slope, and it’s unstable if the graph has a positive slope. So at least for a linear differential equation, the slope determines the stability of an equilibrium. If the slope was negative, the equilibrium was stable, if the slope was positive, the equilibrium was unstable.</p> 

<p>Will that work for a nonlinear function f(x)? Let’s draw the graph of an example f. A nonlinear function of course isn’t as simple as a linear one, because it has different slopes, the slope changes with x. Which slope of the function would be the relevant one for determining the stability of an equilibrium? We simply have to remember that stability is a local property, so that to determine the stability of a particular equilibrium, all we care about is the local behavior of f around that equilibrium, which is well-captured by its tangent line. So if we’re looking at a particular equilibrium, let’s call it x_1, then the slope of the tangent line is just the derivative of f, evaluated at x_1. And it's this slope that will determine the stability of the equilibrium x_1. In this example, f’(x_1) is positive, which implies the equilibrium x_1 is unstable. Which you can verify graphically by looking at the sign of f on either side of x1. F is positive to the right and negative to the left, meaning that the solution x will move away from the equilibrium. Of course the slope at a different equilibrium will be different. In this case, we call the second equilibrium x_2, the slope of the tangent line around x_2, which is the derivative evaluated at x_2. Since this slope is negative, the equilibrium is stable. Which we can also verify by looking at the sign of f on either side of the equilibrium x_2. F is negative to the right and positive to the left, meaning that if f starts close to the equilibrium x_2, either a little bit above or a little bit below, it will move toward the equilibrium. </p>

<p>We can summarize these results with a stability theorem. Let x_e be an equilibrium of the autonomous differential equation dx/dt = f(x). Then, if f’(x_e), the derivative at the equilibrium, is less than 0, the equilibrium is stable. On the other hand, if f’(x_e) is positive, then the equilibrium is unstable. This works in general for any nonlinear function f, as long as the derivative is defined. Unfortunately the stability theorem doesn’t cover one case - and that’s the case if the derivative f’ is 0. If the derivative is 0, the theorem fails to tell us anything. In that case we don’t know the property of the equilibrium. It’s possible the equilibrium is stable, or it might be unstable, or something else. So for our purposes, we’ll just throw up our hands and say we don’t know the answer if we get a 0 derivative at the equilibrium. But as long as the derivative is non-zero, we can determine without graphing the function f whether or not the equilibrium is stable. </p>


</section>
</section>